From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:07:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] socket: reset a guest connection whose host socket hung up

A host socket that hangs up while libslirp cannot read it was handed
to soread() all the same, in two states.

A socket shut down in both directions without an error made soread()
meet end of file, and XNU answers getpeername() with EINVAL there
where only ENOTCONN was known, so the connection was closed rather
than dropped. Once the guest wrote, the socket was polled for writing
only, XNU reported a hang-up at once, and every pass of
slirp_pollfds_poll called soread() again and re-armed the 2MSL timer:
the event loop spun on one core for as long as the connection lived.

A guest that does not take the data in so_snd lets it fill. The socket
is polled for reading only below half full, but for writing while the
guest has data to send, and poll() reports a hang-up whether it was
asked for or not. Between half full and full, soread() was called on
every such pass and read the room there was, as XNU keeps received
data readable after a reset, so the buffer filled within a few passes.
At full, sopreprbuf() found no room and soread() asserted; built
without assertions it called recv() on an uninitialised buffer.

Send a socket that reports a hang-up or an error and cannot be read,
because its end of file was read or so_snd is full, to the new
sohangup(). soread() and sohangup() share one classification: the
errors ECONNABORTED, ECONNRESET, ECONNREFUSED, ENOTCONN and EPIPE
drop the connection, any other closes it in order. Without a pending
error, a stream socket whose peer address is gone is dropped too, as
a TCP socket shut down in both directions hangs up with nothing to
report; the failed send() that put the guest's data aside does not
take the error on XNU.

A Unix-domain socket (slirp_add_unix) loses its peer address on an
orderly close as well, and XNU answers EINVAL there too, so neither
function asks it: the peer's close is a plain end of file, and the
guest still gets everything queued for it, then a FIN. For a close
that cannot yet be read, sohangup() shuts the socket's write side,
which drops what the guest wrote, and soread() ends the connection
once the guest has made room; data the guest sends after that read is
reset, as a closed TCP peer would. The guest's later data for such a
socket is acknowledged and freed in every in-order path of
tcp_input(), as tcp_reass() already did, not appended to so_rcv that
nothing writes, or its window would fall to zero and hold the guest.
At an end of file already read, the guest's side is closed and the
second tcp_sockclosed() only re-arms the linger timer, as in
sowrite()'s disconnect path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
